# Qualcomm Wayland stubs

Qualcomm's EGL Wayland subdriver also implements the GBM backend used by raylib.
Deleting it broke graphics in PR #502, so keep the subdriver and its existing
CommitBuffer fix. Its Wayland protocol paths are unused without a compositor.

build.py compiles stubs.c, renames the Wayland imports in GBM and the EGL subdriver
to private agnos_* symbols, and removes their Wayland and libffi6 dependencies.
The private names prevent these stubs from interposing on real Wayland clients.
Every stub function logs its name and aborts if an unexpected path calls it.
The three protocol interface descriptors only satisfy unused data relocations.
The build fails if a driver gains an import that the stubs do not cover.

The Docker compiler stage generates the patched libraries; no patched graphics
binaries are checked in. Preserve this COPY after userspace/libs, and the final
Adreno symlink fix after ldconfig, so both libgbm.so and libgbm.so.1 use the patch.

Noble Wayland packages remain where other libraries or development headers need
them. The old custom Wayland and libffi6 packages are no longer installed.
Device testing must still cover rendering, calibration, transitions and reboots.

# OpenCL event interop

GLES only looks up cb_get_sync_obj_from_cl_event_qcom in libCB when handling an
OpenCL event. opencl_stubs.c replaces that backend with an aborting stub, and the
unused OpenCL runtime and SDK packages are removed.

Keep libllvm-qcom.so: tinygrad's QCOMCompiler uses it directly to compile kernels,
even when execution uses the QCOM backend instead of the OpenCL runtime.

test.c loads GBM, EGL, GLES and the EGL subdriver with RTLD_NOW, rejects loaded
Wayland/libffi6/OpenCL runtimes, and checks that both disabled entry points abort.
This runs during the image build; GPU rendering still requires device testing.
